{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,26]],"date-time":"2026-08-26T15:26:58Z","timestamp":1787758018240,"version":"build-2784847793"},"reference-count":58,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"31","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,14]],"date-time":"2026-08-14T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1786665600000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":13,"URL":"http:\/\/onlinelibrary.wiley.com\/termsAndConditions#vor"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,1]],"date-time":"2026-08-01T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1785542400000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/doi.wiley.com\/10.1002\/tdm_license_1.1"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["chemistry-europe.onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["ChemistrySelect"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Biomass\u2010derived porous carbon has emerged as a sustainable electrode material for supercapacitor applications. In this work, activated carbon (AC) was prepared from\n                    <jats:italic>Eucalyptus<\/jats:italic>\n                    seeds through a simple KOH activation process using an abundant and renewable biomass precursor. The synthesized carbon exhibited a hierarchical porous structure with a specific surface area of 610.248 m\n                    <jats:sup>2<\/jats:sup>\n                    g\n                    <jats:sup>\u2212<\/jats:sup>\n                    <jats:sup>1<\/jats:sup>\n                    . Electrochemical measurements in 2\u00a0M KOH delivered specific capacitances of 266 F g\n                    <jats:sup>\u2212<\/jats:sup>\n                    <jats:sup>1<\/jats:sup>\n                    in a three\u2010electrode configuration and 120 F g\n                    <jats:sup>\u2212<\/jats:sup>\n                    <jats:sup>1<\/jats:sup>\n                    for the symmetric device in a two\u2010electrode configuration at 0.5 A g\n                    <jats:sup>\u2212<\/jats:sup>\n                    <jats:sup>1<\/jats:sup>\n                    . Dunn's kinetic analysis revealed that charge storage was predominantly governed by surface\u2010controlled capacitive processes, while the porous architecture and oxygen\u2010containing surface functional groups promote efficient electrolyte transport and ion adsorption. The assembled symmetric supercapacitor delivered a maximum energy density of 11\u00a0Wh kg\n                    <jats:sup>\u2212<\/jats:sup>\n                    <jats:sup>1<\/jats:sup>\n                    at a power density of approximately 200\u00a0W kg\n                    <jats:sup>\u2212<\/jats:sup>\n                    <jats:sup>1<\/jats:sup>\n                    . It retained 6\u00a0Wh kg\n                    <jats:sup>\u2212<\/jats:sup>\n                    <jats:sup>1<\/jats:sup>\n                    at approximately 1200\u00a0W kg\n                    <jats:sup>\u2212<\/jats:sup>\n                    <jats:sup>1<\/jats:sup>\n                    , and maintained 76.5% capacitance retention after 10,000 charge\u2013discharge cycles. The device also successfully powered five red LEDs for 13 s after a 10 s charging period, demonstrating its practical energy\u2010storage capability. These findings indicate that\n                    <jats:italic>Eucalyptus<\/jats:italic>\n                    seed\u2010derived AC is a sustainable and promising electrode material for supercapacitor applications.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/slct.74152","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,14]],"date-time":"2026-08-14T15:06:46Z","timestamp":1786720006000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Enhanced Electrochemical Performance of\n                    <i>Eucalyptus<\/i>\n                    Seed\u2013Derived Activated Carbon for Supercapacitors"],"prefix":"10.1002","volume":"11","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5305-0250","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Anitha","family":"Nakka","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Physics National Institute of Technology  Warangal India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Saraswathi","family":"Kailasa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Physics National Institute of Technology  Warangal India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-2795-4800","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Priyanka","family":"Jagnade","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Chemical Engineering National Institute of Technology  Warangal India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3865-5788","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Prabhu","family":"Rajagiri","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Physics B V Raju Institute of Technology Narsapur Medak Telangana India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Akshay Kranth","family":"Allam","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering National Institute of Technology  Tadepalligudem Andhra Pradesh India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4978-7011","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Venkatappa Rao","family":"Tumu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Physics National Institute of Technology  Warangal India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,14]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_2_9_2_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.est.2024.112878"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_3_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.jelechem.2024.118366"},{"key":"e_